# Docker only reads the .dockerignore at the root of the *build context*.
# Both services build with `context: .` (the repo root), so
# kigali-freight-router/.dockerignore was never being applied — it sat
# there looking like it excluded .env, .git and node_modules while the
# build context actually contained all of them. This file is the one that
# takes effect; keep exclusions here, not in the per-service copies.

# Never bake dependencies from the host: both Dockerfiles run `npm ci`
# themselves, and copying a host node_modules risks platform-mismatched
# native builds on top of being slow.
**/node_modules

# Secrets. No .env is present in the copied service directories today, so
# nothing has leaked — but `COPY kigali-freight-router ./` would sweep one
# straight into an image layer the moment someone adds it locally.
**/.env
**/.env.*
!**/.env.example
kigali-freight-router/config/secrets/

# The commit stamp is written inside the image from build args (see the
# router Dockerfile). Copying the host's copy in is what made /health
# report a months-stale SHA with total confidence: the file is gitignored
# and regenerated only by an npm script nothing ever invoked, so every
# rebuild re-baked whatever happened to be lying around.
kigali-freight-router/build-info.json

# Nothing in the build reads git history — the commit is passed as a build
# arg — and this is 12MB of context transferred on every build.
.git
.github

**/npm-debug.log
**/*.log
dist-export
